{:edb/db-before-t 1719 :edb/db-after-t 1720 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535032 50 #inst "2026-10-08T15:16:14.580Z" 13194139535032 true] [17592186327814 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327815 13194139535032 false] [17592186327814 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327817 13194139535032 false] [17592186327814 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327825 13194139535032 true] [17592186327814 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327827 13194139535032 true] [17592186327814 1100 "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{21}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nAt which values of $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3)$ does $f$ have a critical point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f$ has a critical point at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{5}{4}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nDetermine the number of local maxima and minima $f(x)$ has on the interval  $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ has \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local maxima and \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local minima.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}+B{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants, find $A$ and $B.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" 13194139535032 false] [17592186327814 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}$. Find the coefficients $A$ and $B$ in its partial-fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}=\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1},\\qquad x\\ne3,-\\frac12.$$\n\n$A=$ {{field-1}}, $B=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139535032 true] [17592186327814 1145 "Factor the denominator:\n\n$$2x^2-5x-3=(x-3)(2x+1).$$\n\nMultiplying the partial-fraction identity by $(x-3)(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$7=A(2x+1)+B(x-3)=(2A+B)x+(A-3B).$$\n\nMatching coefficients yields $2A+B=0$ and $A-3B=7$. Thus $B=-2A$, so $7A=7$ and\n\n$$A=1,\\qquad B=-2.$$\n\nSubstitution verifies the identity because $(2x+1)-2(x-3)=7$.\n\nIf this decomposition is used to evaluate the improper integral from $4$ to infinity, keep a common finite upper bound $R$ and take the limit only after combining the terms:\n\n$$\\int_4^\\infty f(x)\\,dx=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\left(\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{x-3}-2\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{2x+1}\\right)=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\ln\\frac{9(R-3)}{2R+1}=\\ln\\frac92.$$\n\nThe two separate improper integrals each diverge, so they cannot be subtracted as independently evaluated improper integrals." 13194139535032 true] [17592186327814 1145 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}=\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\n$$\n\nOur task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-3)(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n7=A(2x+1)+B(x-3).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{2}-3) \\\\ 7 & =-\\frac{7}{2}B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot 3+1)+B(3-3) \\\\ 7 & =7A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A=1$ and $B=-2.$" 13194139535032 false] [17592186327825 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327826 13194139535032 true] [17592186327825 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535032 true] [17592186327825 1041 "field-1" 13194139535032 true] [17592186327825 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327826 13194139535032 true] [17592186327825 1110 #uuid "85758217-04a2-5aa3-8ddd-e0c46cc59e54" 13194139535032 true] [17592186327826 1034 "1" 13194139535032 true] [17592186327826 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535032 true] [17592186327826 1111 #uuid "73cf6ac4-f0f0-5512-a57b-e7378900b295" 13194139535032 true] [17592186327827 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327828 13194139535032 true] [17592186327827 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535032 true] [17592186327827 1041 "field-2" 13194139535032 true] [17592186327827 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327828 13194139535032 true] [17592186327827 1110 #uuid "af6489b1-05a1-55da-9195-4e8d26b2515a" 13194139535032 true] [17592186327828 1034 "-2" 13194139535032 true] [17592186327828 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535032 true] [17592186327828 1111 #uuid "f1fea7ea-193d-5611-8661-4485ed158ebf" 13194139535032 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-128339/mathematical-correction-v1/field-1" 17592186327825 "q-128339/m